sizeof(SPRITE) = 519 bytes (packed? In Watcom, if there's a #pragma pack… this struct has BOOLs (1 byte) and no alignment pragmas visible, so under the default pack(8) Watcom will insert padding after each 1-byte field to align to 4). Hmm — with default alignment, each BOOL (1 byte) will be followed by 3 bytes of padding to reach a 4-byte boundary: ground(1)+pad(3), suck(1)+pad(3), shadow(1)+pad(3). So size = 519 + 9 = 528? Let me verify with the data: 69168 / 528 = 131.0? 528 × 131 = 69168 ✓✓✓.

So SPRITE = 528 bytes (with default alignment padding after the three BOOLs). 131 sprites in the SPRITE1_ITM library.

Let me verify with the dump: the first entry starts with `54 41 52 47 54 31 47 31 5f 50 49 43 00 00 00 00` = "TARGT1G1_PIC" ✓ (16 bytes), then `ee 00 01 00` = item=0x100ee=65774?? GLB item ID 0x000100EE — that's a valid item (FILE1 item 0xEE=238). Wait, but in the .INC, TARGT1G1_PIC = 0x10138. Hmm, 0x100EE vs 0x10138. But ENEMY_LoadSprites sets `curlib->item = GLB_GetItemID(curlib->iname)` — the stored item field gets overwritten with the item ID resolved from the name at runtime! So the stored value doesn't matter. ✓ (0x100ee could be from an older build.)

Next bonus: `ff ff ff ff` = -1 = EMPTY ✓. exptype=4 (EXP_GRDMED)… plausible.

FLATSG1_ITM: 5376 bytes / 8 (FLATS = {SHORT linkflat, SHORT bonus, SHORT bounty} = wait: {INT linkflat; SHORT bonus; SHORT bounty} = 8 bytes with alignment) = 672 flats. 5376/8 = 672 ✓.
First entry: linkflat=0, bonus=-1, bounty=1; entry 2: linkflat=1, bonus=-1, bounty=2…

Tile images: STARTG1TILES is a label at item index 0x35f (863), ENDG1TILES is at 0x600 (1536). So tiles = items 863..1535 (673 tiles?). Hmm, 1536-863 = 673. FLATSG1 has 672 entries. tile item = startflat[game] + flats_index, where startflat = GLB_GetItemID("STARTG1TILES") + 1 = 864. So tiles = items 864..864+671 = 864..1535, ending just before ENDG1TILES (1536). ✓

GLB_GetItemID returns a handle = (filenum << 16) | itemnum. For labels, the item number of the label entry itself (which has size 0).

Next up is the sound format. In the FILE0000 dump, the SFX labels (EXPLO_FX at 0x68, etc.) are 0-byte label entries. The actual sound items are between the labels: EXPLO_FX is at item 0x68 (104), EXPLO2_FX is at 0x6D (109) → items 104-108 = 5 sounds in the "AIR EXPLOSION" group (MAX_SFXS = (END-START-1)/5 = 5 per group). GUN_FX is at 0x90 (144) → items 144-148.

